{% aside type="tip" title="NAHPU v1 Tropical Mountains" %}

NAHPU v1, codename Tropical Mountains, is out. It is available now on [Google Play](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.hhandika.nahpu) for Android and on the [Microsoft Store](https://apps.microsoft.com/detail/9nx3z8p3nsml) for Windows. Linux installs from the signed packages below. The same release is out for Apple devices, and its App Store listing will follow soon; until then, install it through TestFlight.

The codename comes from the first real field expedition test of NAHPU, on Mt. Gede, West Java, Indonesia.

{% /aside %}

## Installation guidelines

{% tabs %}

{% tabitem label="Android" %}

Requirements: Android 7.0 (Nougat) or later.

{% storebadge store="google-play" locale="en" /%}

To install the app, follow these steps:

{% steps %}
1. Open the NAHPU listing on [Google Play](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.hhandika.nahpu).
2. Tap **Install** and wait for the download to finish.
3. Once installed, you can find the app in your app drawer or home screen.
{% /steps %}
{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Apple Devices" %}

Requirements:

- iOS 15.6 or later
- iPadOS 15.6 or later
- macOS 12.4 or later (supports arm64 and x86_64)

Apple requires beta testers to use Apple [TestFlight](https://developer.apple.com/testflight/).

Device support:

- The list of oldest supported macOS devices can be found on [Apple Support](https://support.apple.com/en-us/102861).
- Device support for iOS and iPadOS can be found on [iosref](https://iosref.com/ios).

To install the app, follow these steps:

{% steps %}
1. Download and install the TestFlight app from the App Store on your iPhone, iPad, or Mac.
2. Open this link on the device where you installed TestFlight: [install NAHPU](https://testflight.apple.com/join/DRG6yUfb).
3. Follow the TestFlight instructions to install NAHPU, then open it from your home screen or Applications folder.
{% /steps %}
{% /tabitem %}

{% tabitem label="Linux" %}

Requirements: an x86_64 distribution with GLIBC 2.35 or later. Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12, Fedora 36, and RHEL or Rocky Linux 9 and later all meet this.

{% tabs %}

{% tabitem label="Ubuntu and Debian" %}

{% steps %}
1. Download the package.

   ```bash
   curl -LO https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu/releases/latest/download/nahpu-Linux-x86_64.deb
   ```

2. Install it. `apt` pulls in the libraries NAHPU needs, including `ffmpeg` and `pulseaudio-utils` for audio recording.

   ```bash
   sudo apt install ./nahpu-Linux-x86_64.deb
   ```

3. Open NAHPU from your application menu, or run `nahpu` in a terminal.
{% /steps %}
{% /tabitem %}

{% tabitem label="Fedora and RHEL" %}

These steps cover Fedora, RHEL, Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux, and other RHEL rebuilds.

NAHPU signs its RPM packages. Import the signing key once, before your first install:

```bash
sudo rpm --import https://nahpu.app/nahpu.asc
```

The key fingerprint is `6D60 D3B0 D903 58AC 418A 886E 4A7A 5114 7237 7563`, also published at [nahpu.app/nahpu-fingerprint.txt](https://nahpu.app/nahpu-fingerprint.txt).

{% steps %}
1. Download the package.

   ```bash
   curl -LO https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu/releases/latest/download/nahpu-Linux-x86_64.rpm
   ```

2. Check the signature. The output ends with `digests signatures OK` when the package is intact.

   ```bash
   rpm -K nahpu-Linux-x86_64.rpm
   ```

3. Install it.

   ```bash
   sudo dnf install ./nahpu-Linux-x86_64.rpm
   ```

4. Open NAHPU from your application menu, or run `nahpu` in a terminal.
{% /steps %}

Audio recording needs `ffmpeg`, which Fedora packages as `ffmpeg-free`. `dnf` installs it along with `pulseaudio-utils` when they are available; if recording does not work, install them yourself with `sudo dnf install ffmpeg-free pulseaudio-utils`.
{% /tabitem %}

{% tabitem label="Other distributions" %}

Use the portable archive on distributions without `.deb` or `.rpm` packages, such as Arch Linux, openSUSE, and NixOS, or when you cannot install system packages.

{% steps %}
1. Download the archive.

   ```bash
   curl -LO https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu/releases/latest/download/nahpu-Linux-x86_64.tar.gz
   ```

2. Extract it into a folder on your computer.

   ```bash
   tar -xzf nahpu-Linux-x86_64.tar.gz
   ```

3. Install the runtime dependencies. The archive carries no dependency information, so your package manager cannot do this for you. NAHPU needs the GTK 3 runtime, which most desktops already provide, plus `ffmpeg` and the PulseAudio command-line utilities for audio recording.

   ```bash
   # Arch Linux
   sudo pacman -S --needed gtk3 ffmpeg libpulse

   # openSUSE, where ffmpeg comes from the Packman repository
   sudo zypper install libgtk-3-0 pulseaudio-utils ffmpeg
   ```

   On NixOS the archive needs an FHS environment, for example `nix-shell -p steam-run --run "steam-run ./nahpu"`.

4. Run the `nahpu` executable inside the extracted folder.
{% /steps %}

If NAHPU does not start, ask the loader what is missing. Run this from inside the extracted folder:

```bash
ldd nahpu | grep "not found"
```

Each line names a library NAHPU cannot load. Install the package your distribution ships it in, then try again. The command prints nothing when every dependency is satisfied.
{% /tabitem %}

{% /tabs %}
{% /tabitem %}

{% tabitem label="Windows" %}

Requirements: Windows 10 version 1809 (build 17763) or later (x64).

{% storebadge store="microsoft-store" locale="en" /%}

To install the app, follow these steps:

{% steps %}
1. Select the badge above to download the NAHPU installer from Microsoft, or search for NAHPU in the Microsoft Store app.
2. Open the downloaded installer, or select **Get** in the Microsoft Store app, and wait for the installation to finish.
3. Open NAHPU from the Start menu.
{% /steps %}
{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}
